**Short answer:** **No.** The `font‑faces` property is a **MapLibre Native‑only** feature. GL JS does not read it, and copying the block into a GL JS style will be ignored (or will cause a validation error).  

Below is a quick‑reference table, followed by the practical ways you can get the same “offline‑font” experience in a GL JS web app.

| Feature | MapLibre Native | MapLibre GL JS (≥ 5.11) | What you have to do for GL JS |
|---------|----------------|------------------------|------------------------------|
| `font-faces` (explicit mapping of a `text‑font` name → local font file + Unicode range) | ✅ (Android ≥ 11.13, iOS ≥ 6.18) | ❌ (not in the spec, open issue #165) | Use one of the two GL JS alternatives below |
| Omit `glyphs` URL → render everything locally with TinySDF | ❌ (Native still needs a glyph server) | ✅ (since v5.11) | Remove the `glyphs` field from the style, load the fonts you need with normal web‑font mechanisms (`@font-face` or `<link>`). |
| Provide a `glyphs` URL → fetch pre‑generated SDF PBFs | ✅ | ✅ | Host the same PBF tree you already use for Native, or copy an existing public set (Noto, OpenMapTiles, etc.). |
| Per‑range fallback when a glyph PBF fails | ✅ (Native will try the next range) | ✅ (GL JS falls back to local rendering for the missing range) | No extra work – just make sure the font files are available locally. |

---

## Why `font‑faces` can’t be reused in GL JS

* **Different APIs.** `font-faces` lives under the **style spec** for *MapLibre Native* only. GL JS’s style parser simply discards unknown top‑level keys, so the block will be ignored.
* **Different rendering pipelines.** Native maps each code‑point to a glyph that it loads from a **local file** you point to. GL JS, on the other hand, expects **glyph PBFs** served over HTTP **or** (since v5.11) it can generate glyphs on‑the‑fly from any web‑font that the browser can load.
* **No one‑to‑one mapping.** `font-faces` also lets you declare explicit Unicode ranges per file (e.g. “this TTF covers U+0400‑U+04FF”). GL JS’s local‑font fallback does **not** let you restrict a font to a range – the browser’s font‑fallback algorithm decides which font supplies each glyph.

Because of those mismatches, you need to adopt one of the GL JS‑specific approaches described next.

---

## Two ways to get offline‑font rendering in GL JS

### 1️⃣ Omit the `glyphs` field and rely on **local‑font fallback** (recommended for most web apps)

1. **Remove `glyphs` from the style** (or set it to `null`).  
   ```json
   {
     "version": 8,
     "glyphs": null,          // <-- important
     "layers": [ … ]
   }
   ```

2. **Load the fonts you want to use** the same way you would for any web page:
   * **Web‑font files (TTF/OTF/WOFF/WOFF2)** hosted on the same origin (or with proper CORS).  
   * Use `@font-face` in CSS or a `<link rel="preload">`/`document.fonts.load()` if you need to guarantee they’re ready before the map starts.
   ```css
   @font-face {
     font-family: "MyBrand";
     src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
     font-weight: 400;
     font-style: normal;
   }
   @font-face {
     font-family: "MyBrand";
     src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Bold.woff2") format("woff2");
     font-weight: 700;
     font-style: normal;
   }
   ```

3. **Reference those names in `text-font`** exactly as you defined them:
   ```json
   {
     "type": "symbol",
     "layout": {
       "text-field": "{name}",
       "text-font": ["MyBrand Regular", "MyBrand Bold", "Noto Sans Regular"]
     }
   }
   ```

4. **What happens under the hood**  
   * For every code‑point GL JS asks the browser to rasterise the glyph via **TinySDF**.  
   * The browser resolves the `text-font` list exactly like CSS `font-family`: first matching loaded font, then fall‑backs, then the system default.  
   * No network request for glyph PBFs, so the map works completely offline (as long as the font files are cached).

5. **CJK special case** – By default GL JS still uses the `localIdeographFontFamily` path for CJK characters (TinySDF on‑device). If you want those characters to come from a specific on‑device font, set the option when you create the map:
   ```js
   const map = new maplibregl.Map({
     container: "map",
     style: myStyle,
     localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif' // optional
   });
   ```

**Pros**
* No glyph‑server, no PBF generation.
* Works offline after the fonts are cached.
* Same code works on any browser that supports the font format.

**Cons**
* Rendering quality for very large font sizes can be slightly different from the SDF glyphs (tiny‑SDF is still high‑quality, but not identical to the pre‑generated SDFs).
* Scripts that need shaping (Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, Khmer, etc.) still need the RTL plugin or will look broken – see the “RTL & shaping” section below.

---

### 2️⃣ Keep a **glyphs server** and serve pre‑generated **PBFs** (the classic MapLibre way)

If you already have a `font-faces` setup in Native, you probably also have a set of **glyph PBFs** (or you can generate them from the same TTF/OTF files). You can reuse those for GL JS:

1. **Host the PBF tree** exactly as the style expects:  
   ```
   https://my.cdn.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf
   ```
   * `{fontstack}` is the comma‑joined list from `text-font`.  
   * `{range}` is a 4‑digit hex range like `0-255`, `256-511`, … (MapLibre will request the ranges it needs).

2. **Point the GL JS style’s `glyphs` field** at that URL:
   ```json
   {
     "glyphs": "https://my.cdn.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
     …
   }
   ```

3. **Make sure CORS headers** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain) are present, otherwise the browser will block the fetch.

4. **Optional fallback** – If a particular range fails (e.g., you didn’t generate it), GL JS will automatically fall back to the local‑font path for that range (since v5.11). You’ll see a console warning like:
   ```
   Unable to load glyph range 0-255 for font "MyBrand". Rendering codepoint U+00E9 locally instead.
   ```

**Pros**
* Guarantees identical visual output between Native and GL JS (same SDF glyphs, same scaling).
* Works for any script that has a pre‑generated PBF, including CJK (if you *do* want to serve them).

**Cons**
* You need a small HTTP server (or CDN) that can serve the PBF files.
* Still requires a network request the first time a range is needed (though the files are tiny, ~10 KB each).
* Does **not** solve shaping‑dependent scripts (Devanagari, Khmer, etc.) – those still render incorrectly because the engine lacks a shaping engine.

---

## Handling scripts that need shaping (Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari, etc.)

| Script | What you need in GL JS |
|--------|------------------------|
| **Arabic / Hebrew** | Load the **RTL text plugin** (see below) **and** have the glyph PBFs (or local fonts) that contain the correct glyphs. |
| **Devanagari, Khmer, Burmese, etc.** | Even with correct glyphs, MapLibre cannot perform the required ligature shaping. The text will appear as a series of isolated glyphs. The only current workaround is to **pre‑render the label as an image** (e.g., via a server‑side rendering service) and use it as an `icon-image`. |
| **CJK** | No shaping needed; GL JS already renders ideographs locally. If you prefer the exact shapes from a specific Noto CJK font, set `localIdeographFontFamily` as shown above, or serve the CJK glyph PBFs and set `localIdeographFontFamily: false`. |

### Enabling the RTL plugin (Arabic / Hebrew)

```js
import { setRTLTextPlugin } from 'maplibre-gl';

// Load the plugin from a CDN or your own host.
// The third argument (`true`) tells the library to defer map creation until the plugin is ready.
setRTLTextPlugin(
  'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-rtl-text.js',
  () => {
    // Plugin loaded – now you can create the map.
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: myStyle,
      // other options …
    });
  },
  true // defer map creation
);
```

If you are already creating the map elsewhere, you can call `setRTLTextPlugin` **before** the first `new maplibregl.Map()`; the library will automatically pause map initialization until the script loads.

---

## Quick checklist for “offline‑font” parity between Native and GL JS

| ✅ | Item | How to achieve in GL JS |
|---|------|--------------------------|
| 1 | **Same visual font** (e.g., “Noto Sans Regular”) | Load the exact same TTF/OTF via `@font-face` **or** serve the same glyph PBFs. |
| 2 | **No network after first load** | Use the *omit‑glyphs* approach (fonts cached by the browser) **or** cache the glyph PBFs with a Service Worker / HTTP cache. |
| 3 | **CJK rendering** | Keep `localIdeographFontFamily` (default) or set it to a specific on‑device CJK font. Do **not** serve CJK glyph PBFs unless you need a custom shape. |
| 4 | **Arabic / Hebrew** | Load the RTL plugin **and** have the glyphs (or local fonts) for those scripts. |
| 5 | **Devanagari / Khmer** | Accept that native shaping is not supported; use image‑based labels if you need correct rendering. |
| 6 | **Cross‑platform parity** | If you need *exactly* the same SDF glyphs on both platforms, go with the glyph‑server approach (option 2). Otherwise, the local‑font fallback (option 1) is simpler and fully offline. |

---

